From f6d5fbedfdbac37f69717cda0b1ab510d2abf2ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:54:18 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Monitor: an explicit CPU allocation is the purchased share
 exactly, below one processor too

Hyper-V's root scheduler (the NucBox's Windows 11 client host, scheduler type 0x4) does not enforce
per-VM processor limits or reserves: root's sustained native run burned 4000M in 4813 ms at 100% of
one VP and 4824 ms with the VP's Limit/Reservation read back at 16%. The guest app's cgroup quota is
therefore the only CPU cap a purchased share has on that host, and allocatedPolicy refused any
explicit quota below the catalog cpuPercent, so a 1% share of 16 logical processors (16% of one)
could only run at the catalog's whole processor (6.25% of the host).

An explicit load cpu is now applied exactly from 1 up to the online processors * 100. The catalog
cpuPercent stays the runtime's default when nothing is allocated; the catalog's real compute minimum
is its GFLOPS floor, which the node's admission turns into the share it allocates. RAM keeps its
catalog minimum, the manifest/AppID/reports are unchanged, and the load answer states the applied
cpuPercent (the launcher refuses to serve on any other). The VM-bound marker comment now says that
its fallback is not a cap under the root scheduler.
Tests: 1%/2%/4%/21%/25%/13% of 16 LPs, 1% minimum, refusals past the online processors, and the real
load path writing cgroup cpu.max 16000/32000/64000 per 100000.
---
 isolation/m3/monitor/cpu_allowance_test.go | 24 +++++++++++++------
 isolation/m3/monitor/cpu_load_test.go      | 23 +++++++++++++++---
 isolation/m3/monitor/main.go               | 28 +++++++++++++---------
 3 files changed, 54 insertions(+), 21 deletions(-)

